#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/id #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5851" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0127da83-d8a9-5f68-8728-84960d887d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562939} {:answer/id #uuid "0e173416-0c75-5d18-bce7-4c5d09c41710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562971} {:answer/id #uuid "8de506f0-de2f-59b1-b21d-03bf197ef857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563265} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8cc306-1cdc-572a-a0df-a9b3558a8b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563300} {:answer/id #uuid "c14bc6e7-d812-5a5d-9c44-7a8b1c28cda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de506f0-de2f-59b1-b21d-03bf197ef857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563265} :answer-field/id #uuid "cff0f0aa-ed85-53f8-b776-8e22210df82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a9630e4-9e0f-5dfd-9e56-64f09a2aef77" :question/math-academy-id "q-113185" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7{x}^{2}-4)}{(2+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(7{x}^{2}-4)}{(2+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{7{x}^{2}-4}{2+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(7{x}^{2}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d43f1d3-b22d-5867-8307-65c268230204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563082} {:answer/id #uuid "45aa1459-ec91-5d0f-876d-907225b3da01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563101} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0acc42-5d9f-5489-a7cc-ce0889ea4809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563291} {:answer/id #uuid "9befdf58-6818-5dbb-aa85-6ebdf0af33d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563295} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d6d94e-9b07-5aed-9c69-b873f522b3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9befdf58-6818-5dbb-aa85-6ebdf0af33d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563295} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba74a4b-5dd5-593f-9dc9-2bcd454346af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b2994c1-1a8d-5ce0-a865-b7991c7a84f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-4701" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b713942-93ca-5c9d-a3b8-25a3368a267e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563078} {:answer/id #uuid "43ffa1cd-cbee-59b1-8cc1-9119288e7cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563096} {:answer/id #uuid "f18d18db-da1a-55e9-acae-c7369ddbbc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563485} {:answer/id #uuid "f7569312-659f-5e08-868a-4fcca5be3ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563496} {:answer/id #uuid "f76eaca6-edcf-5542-bb9b-594dcc4cca68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b713942-93ca-5c9d-a3b8-25a3368a267e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563078} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e22f3dc-055e-5904-995a-2399c21aaf1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4144a704-615f-5e15-9570-1e799a640e02" :question/math-academy-id "q-6976" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{9-{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{9-{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}-1)}{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}}-1)}{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-1}{2+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f90715f-6590-53b8-a8aa-071189b081c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563052} {:answer/id #uuid "590b713e-277a-5c29-b787-c52ac579d64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563144} {:answer/id #uuid "7413298a-d6e6-5a35-8720-7fdc338bfb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563203} {:answer/id #uuid "c80d4507-8aaf-5cf1-ae74-4294bb3580dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563385} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6e9679-d3bb-579b-8ff5-6e6f354f6e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7413298a-d6e6-5a35-8720-7fdc338bfb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563203} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a9e411-8f65-5124-9c8e-cea45774f41c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69e4acc7-7029-59de-a5d6-0ccfcee72b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-6978" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}}{16{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}}{16{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{3}{x})}{(16-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{3}{x})}{(16-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2+0}{16-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{8}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fea007a-0dd4-5f6c-9257-ed12e9d45202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434773} {:answer/id #uuid "3576ce64-1716-532c-9b43-4e79dd447ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{7}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434774} {:answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434775} {:answer/id #uuid "81a8f7bc-4cfa-55bc-a03d-5635c11ef30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434777} {:answer/id #uuid "bf44ca15-0e20-5367-bb70-8f8828b77a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{10}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d16fda-27d0-5acd-9321-077265d3dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434775} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e0e053-f6d5-5da3-8737-b2a31b0bda6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "240f8824-faa1-5120-9751-29976f966dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-6994" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+5)}{17x+7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{10-3x-{x}^{2}}{17x+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{17x}{x}+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{10}{x}$ and $\\frac{7}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{x}-3-x)}{(17+\\frac{7}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3-x}{17+0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+3}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Denominator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b3a2b1a-ef2a-573d-b248-54b8b0775046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086331} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb53898-ddcd-5243-b851-41eb392bf7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562968} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5db833-4c7c-5ecf-873d-e9c31e077feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563118} {:answer/id #uuid "77829675-a0a6-598c-938f-5c80feb96d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563209} {:answer/id #uuid "bab6c318-2428-544a-9a59-3a20c69e74e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d5db833-4c7c-5ecf-873d-e9c31e077feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563118} :answer-field/id #uuid "8243a955-7658-5fc2-b22c-f540ebc504d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd34e155-3ce2-5319-8a89-39c8d0e8465a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6999" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{2}+4}{x(3{x}^{2}+12)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{2}+4}{3{x}^{3}+12x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{2}+4}{3{x}^{3}+12x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{12x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(3+\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(3+\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"}]}
